<h2>Guarantee 1 — The Jobs In {{ site.guaranteeDays }} Promise</h2>
<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>If your website doesn't generate at least one genuine job enquiry within {{ site.guaranteeDays }} days of going live, we rebuild it from scratch free of charge and keep working until it does.</strong></p>
</div>

<h3>What counts as a genuine job enquiry</h3>
<p>A real prospective customer contacting you about work, through your website. That means a form submission, a tracked phone call from the site, or an email that came from the site.</p>
<p>What doesn't count: spam, bots, sales pitches, recruiters, or enquiries from people who already knew you and would have called you anyway. We're guaranteeing that the website brings you strangers, not that your mates can find your phone number.</p>

<h3>The conditions</h3>
<p>All of these have to be true. They exist because each one is something that, if it doesn't happen, makes the outcome impossible for us to influence:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You provided photos and approved copy within 5 working days of each draft.</strong> We can't hit a timeline you're not on.</li>
  <li><strong>You were on the Get Found Plan for the full {{ site.guaranteeDays }} days.</strong> A site nobody is actively managing can't be guaranteed to rank — Google demotes profiles that go quiet. This is the condition people push back on, and it's the one we won't move on.</li>
  <li><strong>You asked at least three past customers for a Google review after launch.</strong> Reviews are the single biggest local ranking factor. Three is a low bar.</li>
  <li><strong>The site stayed live for the full {{ site.guaranteeDays }} days</strong>, and wasn't modified by anyone other than us, redirected, deindexed or taken down.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What you get if we miss</h3>
<p>A complete rebuild at no cost, and we keep working until you get an enquiry. The remedy is work, not a refund — we'd rather fix the problem than pay you to go away.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Guarantee 2 — The 7-Day Launch Pledge</h2>
<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>Your website will be live within {{ site.buildDays }} of your kickoff call. If we miss that deadline without giving you at least 48 hours' notice, we credit NZ$200 against your invoice.</strong></p>
</div>

<h3>How the clock works</h3>
<p>It runs on working days — weekends and New Zealand public holidays don't count. It pauses while we're waiting on you: if we're sitting on your photos or your copy approval, those days aren't ours.</p>
<p>That's the only fair way to run a fixed timeline, and it's why the 5-working-day approval condition exists on both guarantees. It's not a loophole to wriggle out through — it's the thing that lets us promise seven days at all.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why no one else does this</h2>
<p>Because most agencies can't. An agency building a restaurant site one week and a law firm the next is starting from zero every time — they can't predict the outcome well enough to underwrite it.</p>
<p>We only build for tradies. We know what a plumber's customer searches for, what a sparkie's enquiry form needs to ask, and which suburb pages actually pull work. That narrowness is what makes the risk calculable, and a calculable risk is one you can guarantee.</p>
<p>The honest version: we're a new business, and we're buying our reputation with this. If the guarantee costs us money on the first ten builds, that's the price of proof — and it's a better use of the money than buying ads that claim the same thing.</p>

<h2>What we don't guarantee</h2>
<p>Being straight about the edges matters more than a bigger promise:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A specific ranking position.</strong> Nobody can guarantee a #1 on Google, and anyone who does is either lying or about to redefine "#1" for you.</li>
  <li><strong>A number of enquiries.</strong> We guarantee at least one genuine enquiry, not a volume. Volume depends on your trade, your area and your reviews.</li>
  <li><strong>That you'll win the jobs.</strong> We can get the phone to ring. What happens on the call is yours.</li>
</ul>

<hr />

